{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-inference.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis from heterogeneous demos

When a demo appears unrelated to another by palette, dimensions, or local geometry, first inspect whether each input nevertheless encodes an ordered sequence or lattice that is expanded into a full periodic output. For a periodic candidate, independently derive the period, symbol/color order, direction, and phase/origin from the input markers and output. Verify the complete generated grid against every demo, including row/column transitions; a visually plausible periodic fill is not evidence by itself. Heterogeneity alone is not grounds either to dismiss a shared abstract rule or to repeatedly request demonstrations without a discriminating question.

This is a diagnostic technique, not a solved task-specific mapping. In the reviewed stream, the demos included sparse color tracks and an already periodic output; the test likewise contained diagonal color tracks, but no transfer rule was rigorously established before the next demo request. Future analyses should extract and validate the proposed sequence-to-field mapping before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u091/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis test across heterogeneous demos

When a demo output is a dense repeating field but other examples look unlike it, neither force a shared rule from visual similarity nor dismiss the periodic case as unrelated. Treat the pattern as a testable abstraction: record the output's row/column period, color cycle ordering, direction, and phase/origin; compare these with the input's sparse diagonal tracks, marker sequence, or stripe ordering. Distinguish period from phase (a correct cycle with wrong origin still fails exact replay).

Replay the proposed generator against every complete demo. Include cases where the output is uniform or striped: these may be degenerate instances of a shared periodic generator, or evidence against it, and must not be ignored. A test input that visually resembles a periodic encoding only motivates the hypothesis; it does not establish mapping. If no deterministic cycle/phase rule reproduces all outputs, label it unresolved rather than submitting cosmetic rotations/reversals or repeatedly requesting demos without a discriminating prediction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u091/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u091/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/single-demo-overreach.md", "content": "# Avoiding overreach from a single demo

A one-pair example can show a striking local color change, but does not by itself prove a broad rule such as filling an enclosure, propagating color across an object, or encoding every matching marker. Record the exact changed cells and equally important unchanged candidates before generalizing. Keep separate hypotheses for task class, selector, operation, and output representation; avoid collapsing all four into one intuitive visual story.

After a candidate fails, the failure rejects the entire candidate transformation. Reopen those four components rather than making a cosmetic adjustment. A retry should explain all observed edits and non-edits and, when more than one rule remains plausible, use a demo request only for a concrete distinguishing prediction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u091/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/single-demo-overreach.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: repeated unvalidated submissions after one demo

In this instance, the only demo had a 29×11 input and a 14×11 output, while the test was 4×9. The demo showed a dense structured field, a full-width separator row (color 4), and a uniform region below it; the output preserved the top 14×11 portion while changing its color-1 cells to color 2. This was strong evidence that the task was not a simple mapping of the test's visible 4×4 color-3 motif to a color-2 motif. The test also contained a full-height color-1 divider and color-3 regions, so its partition/representation needed to be inferred via a discriminating additional demo.

The unsuccessful actions repeatedly submitted variants of the same guessed 4×4 output, including an identical grid more than once, and claimed an overlap rule without demo support. After a wrong submission, reopen task class and output dimensions; do not repeat an identical grid or cosmetic variant. If the one demo does not establish how a differently sized test is represented, spend a demo request to resolve the mapping rather than burn attempts on unsupported guesses. Keep claims about the rule out of the response unless evidence validates them. In puzzle mode, still emit exactly one permitted JSON action on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u091/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md"}
